A||N||11||87ECO5||Calling all dog breeders or people with info on the subject||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||11-15-2001||01:17 PM||||Several months ago, I had to put my 8 year old golden retriever (Maxi) down due to an illness.  I miss her terribly but I've decided to get another one after Christmas.  However, I wanted to ask some questions first.<P>The breeders in my area are charging between $300 and $400 dollars for a female GR pup.  In their ads, they all mention things like AKC (certified with pedigrees), champion bloodlines in sire, etc.<P>Can someone tell me what's most important when purchasing from a breeder?  What should I look for and what should I look out for?  I've never dealt with a breeder before (I've always purchased my dogs from the humane society - including Maxi) so I'm looking for pointers.  Maxi was plagued with epileptic seizures - the thing that led to her illness (God bless her, she always had a wire crossed somewhere in that thick head of hers).  I'm hoping that spending the money on a "well-bred" retriever from a breeder will protect her - AND my family and I - from having to go through that (again).<P>Any help would be greatly appreciated.<P>TIA||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||11-15-2001||01:32 PM||||At the risk of annoying any breeders that may be out there, I'll suggest going back to the humane society. I think mutts make the best pets. <BR>Many purebred dogs are inbred to the point that they have lots of genetic problems. Most common is hip displaysia. <BR>If you decide to go to a breeder, at the very least you want to be able to see both parents in their natural environment. <BR>Beware of the "puppy mills".<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||11-15-2001||01:39 PM||||Ditto what Raydar said. There are some problems with pedigree animals- like some labs have a genetic predisposition for cancer. But, in crossed blood line animals, the dominate traits for each breed usually overcomes the shortcomings of the full breed.<BR>Oh, and BTW, I have a really nice Border Collie mix that needs a good home- she is house broke, had some shots and loves to play with my other pets- An Aussie, a beagle mix and the 2 cats.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ray b||11-15-2001||03:09 PM||||as english royals have proved inbreeding is bad.<BR>get a mutt not a inbred dog.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||3
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||11-15-2001||03:31 PM||||The best way to avoid the inbreeding problems is to get the original dog.<P> get a wolf!||||reg||10
Z||000005||ChopTop||11-15-2001||03:31 PM||||Breed specific book are very helpful in explaining the most common problems to look for when you are ready to purchase.  Along with giving tips on what to look for from a breeder.  Do you know if you want pet quality or show quality, as this will help determine the price of dog.  Howver, first you may want to check with your local humane society, purebreed dogs are sometimes turned in when the dog doesn't meet the owners lifestyle.  Also check and see if there are any GR rescue services.  Many breeds have individuals who love the breed so much they literally rescue the dog and keep them until they can find a good home to place them in.||||reg||1
Z||000006||87ECO5||11-15-2001||03:32 PM||||I've got a mut already.  I want another golden retriever.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Somebody pointed me to the AKC and GRCA (Golden Retrievers Club of America) websites for references to good breeders.  Looks like I can expect to pay closer to $700+ for a "properly-bred" GR.||||reg||7
Z||000007||Phil||11-15-2001||04:17 PM||||Let the breeder know that you are looking for a pet not a show dog. Some of the top kennels have a rather large difference in price for pet quality( you may not get papers though)||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||11-15-2001||04:46 PM||||Scott--Anita bought our our German Shepherd from a breeder named Mark Kidd in Wayne Township (West Middletown).  I think he only deals with German Shepherds, but he might know of some good leads for Golden Retrievers.  Anita said he's a real nice guy (she went to school with him).  Email me if you want his phone number (it's in the phone book). ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Mach10||11-15-2001||07:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>At the risk of annoying any breeders that may be out there, I'll suggest going back to the humane society. I think mutts make the best pets. <BR>Many purebred dogs are inbred to the point that they have lots of genetic problems. Most common is hip displaysia. <BR>If you decide to go to a breeder, at the very least you want to be able to see both parents in their natural environment. <BR>Beware of the "puppy mills".<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Agreed. Mutts rule. The cream floats to the top when you mix things. My puppy, Roo, combines all the best features of whatever the hell is in her blood; Labrador, Shep, Rotty, Husky, Someone's leg, etc.<BR>Loveable and affectionate, fast as hell, built like a sherman tank up front, furry paws, cute culry tail, and Denim in color  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||14
Z||000010||Indiana_resto_guy||11-15-2001||07:48 PM||||I know a breeder in Mt. Comfort. Her prices are similar. Here's a pic of mine.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DSC00009_1.JPG"> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||avengador1||11-15-2001||07:51 PM||||Golden retrievers are also known to have hip problems. Like it was said above, a lot of pure bred dog are inbred and have many different problems. Goldens really love attention and are very smart if trained properly. I had one for 12 yrs. and miss him alot but cannot bring myself to get another.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
